            <ArticleTitle>Ras-MEK Signaling Mediates a Critical Chk1-Dependent DNA Damage Response in Cancer Cells.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>694-704</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1158/1535-7163.MCT-16-0504</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Cancer cell line profiling to identify previously unrecognized kinase dependencies revealed a novel nonmutational dependency on the DNA damage response checkpoint kinase Chk1. Although Chk1 is a promising therapeutic target in p53-deficient cancers, we found that Ras-MEK signaling engages Chk1 in a subset of osteosarcoma, ovarian, and breast cancer cells to enable their survival upon DNA damage, irrespective of p53 mutation status. Mechanistically, Ras-MEK signaling drives Chk1 expression and promotes cancer cell growth that produces genotoxic stress that requires Chk1 to mediate a response to the consequent DNA damage. Reciprocally, Chk1 engages a negative feedback loop to prevent hyperactivation of Ras-MEK signaling, thereby limiting DNA damage. Furthermore, exogenous DNA damage promotes Chk1 dependency, and pharmacologic Chk1 inhibition combined with genotoxic chemotherapy potentiates a DNA damage response and tumor cell killing. These findings reveal a mechanism-based diagnostic strategy to identify cancer patients that may benefit from Chk1-targeted therapy. Mol Cancer Ther; 16(4); 694-704. ©2017 AACR.</AbstractText>
                <CopyrightInformation>©2017 American Association for Cancer Research.</CopyrightInformation>
            </Abstract>
